            <p>French food chains faced disruptions due to hacked refrigeration controllers with outdated firmware and insecure network access.</p>
            <p>The Danfoss refrigeration controller vulnerabilities exposed a critical gap in supply chain security. Attackers exploited outdated firmware and poorly secured network access to disrupt food supply chains, causing millions in losses. This isn’t just a technical flaw—it’s a systemic risk in how we manage critical infrastructure.</p>
            <p>The attack highlights the need for better firmware updates and strict network exposure management. Critical systems like refrigeration controllers should never be left exposed to the internet. A single vulnerability can cascade into widespread damage, as seen in France. The solution lies in proactive patching and securing the entire control stack.</p>
            <p>Regulatory frameworks like the Cyber Resilience Act and NIS2 are steps in the right direction, but they’re not yet comprehensive enough. The law ends where liability begins. Manufacturers must take responsibility for insecure software, and operators must enforce strict access controls. This is about more than compliance—it’s about preventing catastrophe.</p>
            <p>The attack also underscores the growing threat of hybrid warfare. State actors are using cyber tactics to create economic and social instability. France’s case fits a broader pattern of targeted disruptions. We need to treat these incidents as signals, not just breaches. The time to act is now.</p>
