            <h3>Unclear AI risk ownership could leave CISOs in familiar scapegoat role</h3>
            <p>CISOs face accountability for AI risks without control over outcomes due to unclear ownership and authority boundaries.</p>
            <p>CISOs are once again finding themselves in the uncomfortable position of being held accountable for risks they may not have control over. AI is amplifying this issue by introducing new complexities that blur traditional lines of ownership. The lack of clear accountability models means security leaders are often left to manage risks without the authority to prevent them. This creates a dangerous precedent where CISOs are expected to own outcomes they can&#x27;t fully control.</p>
            <p>The problem isn&#x27;t just about who owns AI risk—it&#x27;s about ensuring that ownership aligns with authority. If CISOs are to be responsible for AI security, they must have the power to review, approve, and monitor AI systems before deployment. Without this, the CISO&#x27;s role becomes reactive rather than proactive. They need the right to stop insecure systems from being used and to manage access and ongoing monitoring.</p>
            <p>But clarity is essential. AI governance involves more than just security—it touches data, operations, and business decisions. CISOs shouldn&#x27;t be expected to manage all these areas alone. The responsibility must be shared across functions, with clear boundaries. Business leaders own how AI is applied, legal teams handle oversight, and CISOs focus on security. This separation prevents CISOs from being scapegoats for decisions outside their control.</p>
            <p>The key takeaway is simple: accountability must be defined before an incident happens. CISOs need to be involved in AI initiatives from the start, with clear ownership and escalation paths. This ensures security is embedded in the design, not an afterthought. If these conversations aren&#x27;t already happening, it&#x27;s time to push for them. The goal isn&#x27;t to assign blame but to build a system where everyone knows their role.</p>
