            <h3>Analysing a novel malicious redirect and cloaking technique</h3>
            <p>Attackers use trusted search engine redirects and layered cloaking to hide malicious payloads and bypass detection.</p>
            <p>The recent Adception malvertising chain underscores the growing sophistication of browser-based attacks. Attackers are leveraging trusted search engine redirects to bypass traditional detection methods, using them as checkpoints to hide malicious payloads. This technique relies on a layered cloaking approach, making it harder for scanners and reviewers to catch the real intent.</p>
            <p>A Google ad pointing to a Bing search result is just the first layer. The compromised site then checks for specific headers and referrers, ensuring only targeted traffic reaches the final malicious page. This shows how attackers are using trusted domains as shields, making it harder to trace the attack back to its source.</p>
            <p>The final page mimics a legitimate download interface, complete with a misleading command that copies a malicious payload to the clipboard. This level of visual deception highlights the need for real-time detection and deeper browser visibility. Traditional methods often miss these subtle but effective attacks.</p>
            <p>As supply chain security and AI governance become more critical, we must invest in tools that provide full session telemetry and control. This attack chain is a clear example of why real-time monitoring and AI-native security are essential for defending against modern threats.</p>
