            <h3>Your AI Agent Has Your Credentials. What Can It Do With Them?</h3>
            <p>AI agents with over-permissioned credentials pose a security risk by potentially accessing and compromising sensitive systems.</p>
            <p>Authentication is the first line of defense, but it’s not the end of the story. AI agents operate with identities that carry authority, and that authority defines the risk. Over-permissioned agents can act with broad access, turning a simple login into a major breach. The real danger isn’t the credentials themselves—it’s what those credentials can unlock.</p>
            <p>Authorization is where the real work happens. Once an identity is inside, the question isn’t who it is, but what it can do. AI systems often inherit the same patterns as legacy systems, with long-lived credentials and broad permissions. This creates standing privilege that can persist indefinitely, expanding the blast radius of any compromise. The key is to limit what any identity can touch, for how long, and from where.</p>
            <p>Zero trust isn’t just a buzzword—it’s a framework that demands continuous verification. Authentication starts the process, but privilege must be scoped, monitored, and revoked when no longer needed. AI agents, like any other identity, should operate with just-in-time access. This means verifying risk continuously, not assuming trust after the first login. The goal is to keep trust earned, not assumed.</p>
            <p>The challenge lies in visibility. As AI becomes more embedded in operations, the identities supporting them often bypass traditional governance. Service accounts, API keys, and workload identities carry standing privilege, making them high-value targets. Organizations must audit access, understand what each identity can do, and ensure controls are in place to mitigate risk. This isn’t just about security—it’s about control.</p>
          </article>
